Pattern 1 — Array Traversal & Access
Core idea
array
  ↓
visit each element
  ↓
process it
Problems

1. Input n elements and print all elements.

2. Print all elements with their indexes.

3. Print the array in reverse order using indexes.

4. Print only elements at even indexes.

5. Print only elements at odd indexes.

6. Print elements greater than a given value k.

Pattern learned
for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    // process arr[i]
}

This is the foundation of everything that follows.

🟢 Pattern 2 — Accumulator

You've already learned this with numbers.

Now transfer:

number → digits → sum

into:

array → elements → sum
Problems

7. Find the sum of all elements.

8. Find the average of all elements.

9. Find the sum of even elements.

10. Find the sum of odd elements.

11. Find the product of all elements.

12. Find the sum of elements at even indexes.

Pattern
initialize
     ↓
traverse
     ↓
update accumulator
🟡 Pattern 3 — Counter + Conditional Traversal
Problems

13. Count positive, negative and zero elements.

14. Count even and odd elements.

15. Count elements greater than k.

16. Count elements smaller than k.

17. Count elements divisible by 3.

18. Count prime numbers in the array.

19. Count perfect-square elements.

Pattern
Traversal
    ↓
Condition
    ↓
Counter++

This connects directly with the logical-loop patterns you learned earlier.

🟡 Pattern 4 — Min / Max Tracking

This is a very important DSA pattern.

Problems

20. Find the maximum element.

21. Find the minimum element.

22. Find the index of the maximum element.

23. Find the index of the minimum element.

24. Find the difference between maximum and minimum.

25. Find the sum of all elements except the maximum.

26. Find the sum of all elements except the minimum.

Pattern
current best
     ↓
compare
     ↓
update

This is where you learn state tracking.

🟠 Pattern 5 — Searching

Now we ask:

"Does this value exist?"

Problems

27. Check whether a given x exists.

28. Find the first occurrence of x.

29. Find the last occurrence of x.

30. Count occurrences of x.

31. Find the index of x.

32. Check whether all elements are unique.

Pattern
Traversal
    ↓
Comparison
    ↓
Decision / Position / Count

This will become extremely useful later when we learn linear search and binary search.

🟠 Pattern 6 — Transformation

Now we're no longer just reading the array.

We're creating a changed version.

Problems

33. Create a new array containing squares.

34. Create a new array containing cubes.

35. Create a new array containing only even elements.

36. Replace every negative number with 0.

37. Replace every even number with 1 and odd number with 0.

38. Copy one array into another manually.

Pattern
Original Array
      ↓
Process
      ↓
New Array

This is an important transition toward more advanced array problems.

🔵 Pattern 7 — Position Manipulation

Now we start working with indexes and positions.

Problems

39. Swap the first and last elements.

40. Swap the first and second elements.

41. Swap alternate elements.

42. Reverse the array without a built-in method.

43. Rotate the array one position left.

44. Rotate the array one position right.

Pattern
Index manipulation
       +
Variable swapping

And this is where your previous Two Pointer learning becomes useful.

For example:

left                    right
 ↓                        ↓
[10, 20, 30, 40, 50]

Reverse:

swap
left++
right--

So your old pattern isn't wasted—it is now being applied to collections.

🔵 Pattern 8 — Array Comparison

Now we work with two collections.

Problems

45. Check whether two arrays are exactly equal.

46. Check whether two arrays have the same elements in the same order.

47. Check whether two arrays contain the same elements ignoring order.

48. Find common elements between two arrays.

49. Find elements present in the first array but not the second.

50. Find the element-wise sum of two arrays.

🔴 Pattern 9 — Frequency & Duplicates

This is the final basic-array pattern before we move toward real DSA techniques.

Problems

51. Count how many times a given element appears.

52. Print the frequency of every distinct element.

53. Print all duplicate elements.

54. Print all elements that occur exactly once.

55. Find the most frequent element.

56. Find the least frequent element.

This section will eventually connect directly to:

Array
  ↓
Frequency
  ↓
HashMap

And since you've already started learning HashMap, this will be an important bridge.

🏁 Pattern 10 — Basic Logical Array Problems

Finally, combine the patterns.

Problems

57. Check if the array is sorted in ascending order.

58. Check if the array is sorted in descending order.

59. Find the second largest element.

60. Find the second smallest element.

61. Count how many elements are greater than the average.

62. Count how many pairs have a given sum k.

